        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="best-design-webflow">#1 — Webflow (Best for Designers & Developers)</h2>
      <p>Webflow is the best WordPress alternative for designers and developers who want pixel-perfect visual design control without writing PHP or managing servers. Webflow's visual canvas generates clean, semantic HTML/CSS/JS — you design in a visual interface, and Webflow outputs production-ready code. For teams that find WordPress themes too restrictive but find raw coding too slow, Webflow is the sweet spot.</p>
      <p>The trade-off is the learning curve. Webflow's visual interface is essentially a visual code editor — understanding CSS box model, flexbox, and interactions is required. The CMS plan at $23/month is needed for content-driven sites. See <a href="/reviews/wordpress-vs-webflow/">WordPress vs Webflow</a> for the full comparison.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>Webflow</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Visual design canvas</strong> — Pixel-perfect design that outputs clean HTML/CSS</li>
          <li><strong>CMS</strong> — Structured content types with designer-controlled templates</li>
          <li><strong>Interactions</strong> — Scroll, hover, and load animations without code</li>
          <li><strong>Hosting included</strong> — AWS-powered CDN, SSL, and automatic scaling</li>
          <li><strong>Basic at $14/mo</strong> — Static sites; CMS at $23/mo for content-driven sites</li>
        </ul>
        <!-- Affiliate CTA position reserved -->
        <a href="https://webflow.com" class="btn-alt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow sponsored">Try Webflow Free</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="best-templates-squarespace">#2 — Squarespace (Best for Beautiful Templates)</h2>
      <p>Squarespace is the best WordPress alternative for non-technical users who want the most beautiful templates out of the box. Squarespace's template designs are the most polished in this list — every template is award-winning, mobile-responsive, and ready to publish. For creatives, photographers, restaurants, and small businesses where aesthetics matter, Squarespace delivers a professional site in hours, not weeks.</p>
      <p>The trade-off is customization depth. Squarespace's drag-and-drop is less flexible than Webflow's visual canvas or WordPress's full code access. You cannot modify the underlying HTML/CSS beyond custom CSS injection. See <a href="/reviews/squarespace-vs-wix/">Squarespace vs Wix</a> for the comparison.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>Squarespace</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Award-winning templates</strong> — Best-in-class designs for every industry</li>
          <li><strong>Drag-and-drop editor</strong> — Visual editing with layout flexibility</li>
          <li><strong>Built-in features</strong> — Email campaigns, analytics, and ecommerce included</li>
          <li><strong>24/7 support</strong> — Live chat and email support on all plans</li>
          <li><strong>Personal at $16/mo</strong> — Unlimited bandwidth and storage</li>
        </ul>
        <!-- Affiliate CTA position reserved -->
        <a href="https://squarespace.com" class="btn-alt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow sponsored">Try Squarespace Free</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="best-beginners-wix">#3 — Wix (Best for Beginners & Drag-and-Drop)</h2>
      <p>Wix is the best WordPress alternative for absolute beginners who want the simplest drag-and-drop website builder. Wix's ADI (Artificial Design Intelligence) builds a starter site from a questionnaire, and the editor lets you drag any element anywhere on the page — no grid constraints. For someone who has never built a website, Wix is the fastest path to a live, professional-looking site.</p>
      <p>The trade-off is SEO and scalability. Wix's SEO has improved but still trails WordPress, Webflow, and Squarespace for complex sites. The app marketplace is smaller than WordPress's plugin ecosystem. Migrating away from Wix is difficult. See <a href="/reviews/squarespace-vs-wix/">Squarespace vs Wix</a> and <a href="/reviews/wix-vs-wordpress/">Wix vs WordPress</a> for the comparisons.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>Wix</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>ADI site builder</strong> — AI generates a starter site from a questionnaire</li>
          <li><strong>Drag-and-drop editor</strong> — Place any element anywhere, no grid constraints</li>
          <li><strong>900+ templates</strong> — Pre-built designs for every industry</li>
          <li><strong>Wix Velo</strong> — Developer platform for custom web apps</li>
          <li><strong>Business Basic at $17/mo</strong> — Includes custom domain, no Wix ads</li>
        </ul>
        <!-- Affiliate CTA position reserved -->
        <a href="https://wix.com" class="btn-alt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow sponsored">Try Wix Free</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="best-interactive-framer">#4 — Framer (Best for Modern Interactive Sites)</h2>
      <p>Framer is the best WordPress alternative for designers who want to build modern, interactive websites with animations, transitions, and responsive design — without writing code. Framer's design-to-publish workflow is the fastest in this list: design in a Figma-like interface and publish to a live URL with one click. For landing pages, portfolios, and marketing sites, Framer is the most modern tool.</p>
      <p>The trade-off is scope. Framer is best for marketing sites and landing pages, not content-heavy blogs or large ecommerce stores. The CMS is basic compared to Webflow or WordPress. See <a href="/reviews/framer-vs-webflow/">Framer vs Webflow</a> for the comparison.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>Framer</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Design-to-publish</strong> — Figma-like interface with one-click publishing</li>
          <li><strong>Animations</strong> — Scroll, hover, and page transitions without code</li>
          <li><strong>Responsive design</strong> — Breakpoints for desktop, tablet, and mobile</li>
          <li><strong>CMS</strong> — Basic content management for blogs and portfolios</li>
          <li><strong>Mini at $10/mo</strong> — 1 project, custom domain</li>
        </ul>
        <!-- Affiliate CTA position reserved -->
        <a href="https://framer.com" class="btn-alt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow sponsored">Try Framer Free</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="best-ecommerce-shopify">#5 — Shopify (Best for Ecommerce)</h2>
      <p>Shopify is the best WordPress alternative for ecommerce. While WooCommerce (WordPress's ecommerce plugin) is powerful, it requires server management, plugin maintenance, and hosting costs. Shopify removes all of that — hosting, SSL, payment gateway, and security are included. For a small business that wants to sell online without WordPress's technical overhead, Shopify is the strongest ecommerce platform.</p>
      <p>The trade-off is content marketing. Shopify's blogging and CMS capabilities are weaker than WordPress. If your site is 80% blog and 20% store, WooCommerce or a WordPress + Shopify hybrid may be better. See our <a href="/reviews/shopify-vs-woocommerce/">Shopify vs WooCommerce</a> comparison.</p>

      <div class="product-box">
        <h2>Shopify</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>All-in-one ecommerce</strong> — Hosting, SSL, payments, and security included</li>
          <li><strong>12,000+ apps</strong> — Largest ecommerce app ecosystem</li>
          <li><strong>Shopify Payments</strong> — Native payment gateway, no extra transaction fees</li>
          <li><strong>POS integration</strong> — Sync online and in-person inventory</li>
          <li><strong>Starter at $39/mo</strong> — Unlimited products, 24/7 support</li>
        </ul>
        <!-- Affiliate CTA position reserved -->
        <a href="https://shopify.com" class="btn-alt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow sponsored">Try Shopify Free</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="selection-scenarios">Selection Scenarios</h2>

      <div class="use-case">
        <h3>Designer / Agency</h3>
        <p><strong>Recommendation: Webflow ($14–$23/mo).</strong> Pixel-perfect design control with clean code output. For faster, animation-heavy sites, Framer is a strong alternative. For clients who need a blog, pair Webflow CMS with a headless approach.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="use-case">
        <h3>Small Business / Creative</h3>
        <p><strong>Recommendation: Squarespace ($16/mo).</strong> The most beautiful templates with the least effort. If you need ecommerce, Squarespace Commerce at $27/mo includes 0% transaction fees.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="use-case">
        <h3>Absolute Beginner</h3>
        <p><strong>Recommendation: Wix ($17/mo).</strong> The simplest drag-and-drop editor. Use ADI to generate a starter site, then customize. If you outgrow Wix, migrate to Squarespace or Webflow.</p>
      </div>

